WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.320
- [Philip] When I was just a newborn,

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.540
my grandmother generously
gifted me a certificate

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.410
for a single share of
Disney stock to celebrate.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.540
The price? $1.40.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.520
- [Julia] Decades later,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.960
we fished that certificate out of our safe

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.780
and did a little research.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.690
Turns out, that little single
share had made some friends:

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.000
through multiple stocks splits
and dividend investment,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.780
we now owned over 50 shares.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.950
On top of that, the share price
had grown to about $35 each.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:36.600
So, in 2007, we sold the
shares for around $1,600,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.320
which we ended up putting towards

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.480
the down payment on our first house.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.627
- [Philip] All told, I
realized a whopping 114,100%

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.420
return on that investment.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.720
Way to go, baby Philip!

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.210
- Okay, Mr. Boss Baby, don't forget.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.160
That was thanks to your Nana,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.580
but now that you can tie your own shoes

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.220
and have all these fancy
financial licenses,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.210
do you have what it takes to pick a winner

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.790
like she did all those years ago?

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.730
- Not only did I learn to tie my shoes,

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:05.810
thank you very much.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.060
But I have also learned
that there's a lot more

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.280
to picking stocks

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.130
than following a gut instinct

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.700
or what I see on Reddit.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.830
Today, we're going to explore the tools

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:19.550
you can use to become your
very own baby Philip moneybags.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.501
Mustache twirling required.

00:01:21.501 --> 00:01:24.751
(playful upbeat music)

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.910
- Let's say a certain
company has caught your eye.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.960
Maybe they make a product you love,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.400
maybe you keep seeing their
name pop up on WallStreetBets,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.610
or maybe you simply work for the company

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.350
and have an opportunity
to become a shareholder.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.680
Before tapping that Buy
button on your trading app,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.840
it might be a good idea to
kick the tires, so to speak.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.140
- Most methods of analyzing stocks

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.120
fall into two major categories:

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.790
technical analysis and
fundamental analysis.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.920
They're often portrayed as locked

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.290
in an eternal battle for supremacy;

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.410
same goal, different tactics.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.580
So, which camp comes out on top?

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.270
Let's start by looking at the technician.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.670
- If you've ever seen a
day trader at a coffee shop

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.100
or those huge stock terminals on TV

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.030
with squiggly lines in
red and green columns,

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.120
then you've seen technical
analysis in action.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.720
This discipline focuses
on tracking price movement

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.480
and trading volume of a company's stock.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.210
What they're looking at
are historical patterns,

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.650
patterns that will clue
them into opportunities

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.530
to sell or buy a stock at
a profit based on the data.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.520
This often requires extensive research

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.835
of past trends,

00:02:41.835 --> 00:02:43.670
sophisticated trading models,

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.600
and a healthy dose of technology

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.730
to execute when they recognize

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.400
the signal they're looking for.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.220
- These repeatable patterns

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.440
are discovered through
something called back-testing.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.650
Back-testing is a critical
concept to the technician.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.970
It suggests that specific movements

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.920
of the stock price or trading volume

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.170
will be repeated in the future.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.440
Here's an example of the cup and handle.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.050
Here's a candlestick

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.770
and, of course, the falling knife.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.570
Many traders develop entire strategies

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:18.050
around a single pattern or
a combination of multiples.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.770
- But mapping the stock price alone

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.850
isn't the only factor they consider.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.440
Another is the moving average.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.640
This is a constantly
updating average price

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.260
or trading volume of a stock.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.190
One classic use of this
particular indicator

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.700
is the crossover strategy.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.840
This strategy looks for a share price

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.590
to fall below or climb
above moving average,

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.270
hence, crossover.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.550
They use these shifts as a signal

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.960
to buy or sell out of their position.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.170
But crossovers are just the beginning.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.600
Strategies like pennants,
flags, or triangles

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.986
are other configurations
analysts might look for.

00:03:56.986 --> 00:03:57.819
(sighs)

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:00.210
Now that our brains
are good and scrambled,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.040
let's visit the other side,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.470
the fundamentalists.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.890
- Fundamental analysts aren't concerned

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.380
with all the squiggly lines

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.070
and fine details of a stock's movement.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.760
Instead, fundamentalists
focus on the overall strength

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.890
and financial stability of a company.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.190
They ask questions like, how
profitable were they last year?

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.560
Are they in a strong position

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.640
to weather an economic downturn?

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.590
Are they currently undervalued?

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.170
Obviously, you can't just
set a meeting with the CFO

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.760
to get answers to these questions.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.060
So, these investors rely
on a few specific formulas.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.470
- If you were to do an online
search for any company stock,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.810
you'll likely come across two essential,

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.490
fundamental indicators:

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.980
earnings per share or EPS,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.869
and price-to-earnings or P/E.

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:48.900
EPS for a company is found

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.480
by taking the company's net income,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.470
subtracting preferred dividends, if any,

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.230
then, dividing that number

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.580
by the number of shares outstanding.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.140
The higher the EPS,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.030
the more profitable the company.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:03.900
Then, if you take their EPS

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.470
and divide it into the
current stock's share price,

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.200
you'll have your P/E ratio.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.120
- These ratios will allow
you to compare a stock,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.620
apples to apples

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.970
or Apple to Samsung.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.290
For example, let's say you're
interested in two companies,

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.290
Donut Dragons and Cupcake Queens.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.750
After you run the numbers,

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.000
you see that Donut
Dragons has a higher EPS

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.290
than Cupcake Queens,

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.680
suggesting a higher profitability.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.170
Further crunching reveals
that Donut Dragons

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.750
also has a lower P/E
ratio than Cupcake Queens.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.510
Meaning, it might be a cheaper buy.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.890
Analysts use all kinds of
creative ratios like this

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.170
to help measure the fundamental value

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.120
of one company over another.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.060
- I'll admit, the idea
that I can just use math

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.670
to make money is really appealing.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.240
It gives the individual a sense of control

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.430
over something as uncontrollable
as an economic market.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.990
And even though these two analytical paths

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.420
can help you make a
more informed decision,

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.400
we can't forget that there
are important aspects

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.300
to company's health that
are neither technical

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.150
nor fundamental in nature.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.970
- These are known as qualitative elements.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:18.190
Does the company have a
durable, competitive advantage?

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.270
How's the management at the top?

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:21.740
What's their turnover like?

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.200
What direction does
this company's industry

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.760
seem to be heading?

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.150
Even if you're able to
find some of this out,

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.210
the information is going to be interpreted

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.320
through the lens of
your personal feelings.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.960
Maybe you feel that a
company, while booming now,

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.230
is ultimately doomed
due to climate change.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.530
Maybe after hearing about
a leadership scandal,

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:42.840
you decide to sell,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.010
despite what the P/E ratio might tell you.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.240
That's okay.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.310
You should allow space for your excitement

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.860
and personal values

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.500
to have a seat at the
decision-making table.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.950
- So, which of these
philosophies ultimately wins out?

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.230
Should you lean into technical analysis

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.130
or focus on fundamentals?

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:02.700
Maybe qualitative research

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.050
is ultimately the most important.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.510
For many investors,
professional and novice alike,

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:09.940
These different approaches

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:12.180
actually aren't in a battle at all.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.480
Instead, when used in tandem,

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.050
they compliment each other.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:19.090
- In 2019, Warren Buffett
told his shareholders

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.227
his three general rules for
choosing a company to invest in.

00:07:23.227 --> 00:07:25.950
"First, they must earn good returns

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.690
on the net tangible capital
required in their operation.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:33.200
Second, they must be run by
able and honest managers.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.840
Finally, they must be
available at a sensible price."

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.470
As you can see, even the Oracle of Omaha

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.720
uses a three-pronged approach.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.950
- Now, if all this makes
your head spin, that's okay.

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.080
This kind of stuff isn't for everyone

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:49.610
and you don't have to do it

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.030
in order to be a successful investor.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.030
But, if solving this puzzle

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:57.580
and picking stocks is of interest
to you and you have time,

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:58.520
go for it!

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.980
Start small and do your own work

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:03.790
to verify anything you
might see on the internet.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:05.000
- The point of all of this

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.870
isn't to become some sort
of magical stock soothsayer

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.740
impervious to losses,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.440
rest assured, there is no such thing.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.800
The goal is to make you a more confident

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.357
and informed investor.

00:08:17.357 --> 00:08:19.470
- [Julia And Philip] And
that's our two cents.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.100
- Quick! Before you go,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.410
do you know what you
wanna be when you grow up?

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:24.630
- No matter your answer,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.400
we think you should check
out "Future of Work,"

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.290
a new six-part docuseries on PBS Voices

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:31.790
hosted by Julia and me

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.850
that explores the real,
big, work-related questions

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.900
that Americans are facing today.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.520
Everything from debt and the gig economy,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.820
to career identity and so much more.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:42.653
